  <title>5.13.2: Creating Exponential Functions from Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01481</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.2: Creating Exponential Functions from Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d819ee6c-b410-4fb3-a183-516954e021cf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>Examine the graph representing an exponential function \(f\). The function \(f\) gives the value of a computer, in
  dollars, as a function of time, \(x\), measured in years since the time of purchase. </para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL DECAY FUNCTION, WHERE VALUE IN DOLLARS IS A FUNCTION OF TIME. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS 0 COMMA FOUR HUNDRED, 2 COMMA 100, AND 4 COMMA 25."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cd2c8b301539b02974ca385d8f1e5d472bcb8440"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Based on the graph, what can you say about the following?</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> The purchase price of the computer</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>$400 </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The value of \(f\) when \(x\) is 1 </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(f(1)=200\) </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> The meaning of \(f(1)\) </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>The value of the computer after 1 year </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> How the value of the computer is changing each year </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>The computer loses half of its value each year. </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>An equation that defines \(f\). (If needed, use the '^' symbol to enter an exponent.)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(f(x)=400(\frac{1}{2})^x\) or equivalent (i.e., \(f(x)=400 \cdot (0.5)^x\)). </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Whether the value of \(f\) will reach 0 after 10 years</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>No, it will not. After 10 years, the value of the computer will be a little less than $0.40. </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><para>For questions 7 - 10, examine the graphs of the exponential functions.</para>
<para>Examine the graph of the exponential function below.
  <figure><media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTION. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS 0 COMMA 5, 1 COMMA 15, AND 2 COMMA 45."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/706e82e99b6d42e7d57b030e561e0396529f0669"/></media></figure></para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Write an equation that defines the function. Use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(f(x)=5\cdot 3^x\) </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>For each, write an equation that defines the function and find the value of the function when \(x\) is 5.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(f(5)=1,215\) </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><para>Examine the graph of the exponential function below. <figure><media alt="GRAPH OF AN EXPONENTIAL DECAY FUNCTION. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS NEGATIVE 1 COMMA 40, 0 COMMA 20, AND 2 COMMA 5."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2cc8a01ad67c48ca53839eefddc5c83144c7167b"/></media></figure></para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Write an equation that defines the function. Use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>compare your work.
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(f(x)=20 \cdot (\frac{1}{2})^x\) (or equivalent, i.e., \(f(x)=20 \cdot (0.5)^x\)). </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>For each, write an equation that defines the function and find the value of the function when \(x\) is 5.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(f(5)=0.625\) </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
